# __PROJECT_NAME__

A Swift web application built with Ignite, Vapor, shared S5 API contracts, and Fluent.

Requires Swift 6.2 or later (Xcode 26.2+ on macOS; CI uses Swift 6.2 on Linux). Run commands from this directory.

```sh
swift run Frontend
swift run Server serve --hostname 127.0.0.1 --port 8080
```

Open http://localhost:8080. The sample is a **public shared notebook**; it has no login or per-user isolation.
It displays the latest 100 notes. Add authentication and authorization before handling private data.

If you have the `s5` executable on your PATH, `s5 dev` runs both commands. Restart after editing Swift or browser assets;
there is no file watcher. `s5 build --release` creates optimized binaries and the generated `Public/` directory.

## Structure

| Location | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `Sources/AppContract` | Codable DTOs, endpoint input/output types, validation, and stable wire names |
| `Sources/Frontend` | Ignite site, layout, and pages |
| `Assets` | Browser interactions and CSS, copied to Public by Ignite |
| `Sources/ServerApp` | Vapor routes and server-only Fluent models/migrations |
| `Sources/Server` | Server lifecycle |
| `Tests/ServerAppTests` | SQLite integration tests |

`Public/s5/client.mjs` is generated from the Swift endpoint catalog. Browser code imports it and calls
`api.createNote({ text: "Hello" })`. Swift clients use `APIClient.call(NotesAPI.Create.self, input: .init(text: "Hello"))`.
The browser runs JavaScript; it does not run Swift or inherit compile-time Swift type checking. All requests are
decoded and validated by Vapor using the shared contract. Swift clients and server handlers are checked at compile time.

## Database and migrations

Local development uses `db.sqlite` and applies migrations at startup. Set `DATABASE_PATH` to relocate SQLite.
Set `DATABASE_URL` to a PostgreSQL URL to use PostgreSQL instead. Use a persistent database and appropriate TLS for deployment.

Production mode does not automatically migrate. Apply migrations as a separate deployment step:

```sh
swift run Server migrate --env production --yes
swift run Server serve --env production --hostname 0.0.0.0 --port 8080
```

`/health` reports that the process is alive. `/ready` also checks the notes database table.

## Container development

The Dockerfile builds both Ignite assets and the Vapor executable. It requires a remote S5 dependency;
an absolute `--s5-path` dependency outside the build context is only for local development.
For reproducible builds, pin S5 to a commit with `s5 new --revision <sha>` and commit `Package.resolved`.

```sh
docker compose build
docker compose up -d db
docker compose run --rm app migrate --env production --yes
docker compose up -d app
```

The Compose database password and disabled TLS are for local container development only. Configure credentials,
networking and TLS through your deployment environment. Set `SITE_URL` when building the frontend for production metadata, or pass `--build-arg SITE_URL=https://example.com` to the container build.
If using SQLite in the container instead, mount a persistent volume at `/data` (the default `DATABASE_PATH` is `/data/db.sqlite`).

## Tests

```sh
swift test
```

Nido remains a separate IaC project. This application does not import it, provision resources, or embed cloud credentials.
Nido or any other deployment tool may deploy the image and provide the database URL.

Endpoint versions are explicit. For incompatible input/output changes, add a new version and retain the old route
while deployed clients need it. Sharing source types does not prove compatibility with previously released clients.
